Drives runtime/gc/gcworld.bit's stop-the-world World/Mutator registry (#1575)
through its own entry points, not rooting. Every operation on the registry is
`@nosplit` — by definition it never polls for the collector — and the program is
straight-line (no loop), so codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` nowhere and a stack
walk would observe nothing either way. Its evidence is the linked binary's
atomic verdicts — whether `worldRendezvous` proceeds past a blocked mutator and
abandons on a running one — which is exactly the ABI.md §5 property the host
collector running is beside the point for.

`BIT_GC=stress` does not report zero, though: 5/5 runs measured
`collections=101`, matching stress.bit's own documented scheduler-loop floor
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`, which every worker OS thread
keeps running regardless of what this program does). None of those 101 pass
through this program's own `@nosplit` code, so a stack walk during one of them
would still observe nothing this program's own logic produced.

This file used to end by claiming "the `world` slice is held live in `main`
regardless, which is what keeps the raw pointers into it valid under
BIT_GC=stress". That was FALSE, and it was #1991. The block was a managed
`[]i64`, section G binds it as the PROCESS-WIDE registry through `worldBind`,
and nothing ever unbinds it — so the live runtime kept reading it after `main`
returned and the slice had been swept, taking a SIGSEGV at teardown with every
assertion already printed correctly. Holding it live through all of `main` does
not help; the fault is after `main` returns. The block is module state now,
which the collector never reclaims and which outlives `main`.
